{"kind":"report","text":"DEPLOYMENT ROUND 13 CHANGES-REQUESTED at exact clean head fbed0df7020b61f275eff622557c457cc7933d0f, against request 3c31955d15c73729a15b35c04e2fe183a9c337eb and promise ada9c0e4853f3a4be1747535170a3ce666227d8a. The prior three blockers are addressed in intent: the entry now names base/profile/root roles, candidate objects are normalized before admission, aliases are retained in the record, case 4 asserts inbox absence, and parent order/root marking are stated. Diff check passes.\n\nFour blockers remain. First, the proposed profile-retention edge cannot exist. Git commit parents must be commit objects; at this exact head, `git commit-tree HEAD^{tree} -p 68cc9efe05d4d91e7c306db3f7b8536f1f388c09 -m profile-parent-probe`, where that ID is the note's blob, fails with `not a valid 'commit' object`. Put the profile blob in a retained tree or parent a profile commit. Then close the second-order issue the request anticipated: a rules blob alone is executable only if its schema/interpreter and referenced material are also fixed and available. Define a self-contained declarative profile plus stable interpreter contract, or retain a content-addressed executable bundle; do not merely move the missing dependency behind a blob ID.\n\nSecond, `base=P` is still an unauthenticated assertion by the drain for an all-refused batch. An admitted batch mechanically constrains P as the parent of its first event, but an all-refused batch has no event and omits E=P, so any ancestor hash can be written in the header and replayed against. Bind base in the topology or derive it from the preceding accounted ledger frontier; specify the first-entry case where no predecessor exists. `entries=n` should be removed unless it adds an independent check: candidate-parent/line bijection already detects truncation and padding, while a redundant drain-authored count can only disagree with the same evidence.\n\nThird, cross-actor aliases and `refs=` are underspecified and unsafe as text. Another submitter can point an inbox ref in their actor namespace at a victim's valid candidate. Object-ID collapse then mixes an unauthorized naming ref into the victim candidate's terminal record, without saying whether the valid candidate is admitted, the alias is refused, or both. Validate every naming ref against the actor/genesis/namespace/key recomputed from the candidate before normalization and give noncanonical/cross-actor aliases their own terminal outcome. Also replace comma joining: comma (and `=`) are legal ref-name bytes, so `refs=a,b` is not injective. Use length-prefixing or a canonical structured encoding and test delimiter-bearing refs.\n\nFourth, `root=true` plus fast-forward-only does not prove there is only one root. A buggy drain can append a commit marked root=true while keeping the old ledger head as a non-first parent; the branch remains a descendant, but the prescribed first-parent walker stops early and hides prior entries. Require a mechanically unique root—preferably a deterministic genesis ledger commit—or make the auditor reject any root marker that has a reachable ledger predecessor anywhere in its parent graph, with a hostile second-root/old-head-as-side-parent probe. Parent-role validation must also distinguish profile commit, E and candidate roles rather than trust position labels alone.\n\nThe revised alias case has a canonical positive control, but the next version must add cross-actor canonical+alias, comma/equal ref names, and second-root-side-parent hostile cases. Approval remains exact-head only.","body":{"head":"fbed0df7020b61f275eff622557c457cc7933d0f","probe":"git commit-tree rejects blob parent 68cc9efe05d4d91e7c306db3f7b8536f1f388c09","verdict":"changes-requested"}}